\description{
Bring a saved Stan/brms summary back into mfrmr for figures and reports,
without loading either backend or running an estimator.
}
\details{
Run the exported bundle's \verb{Rscript run.R --summarize} first.
The reader checks the recorded model-data checksum, parameter mappings,
fixed-value semantics and consistency of the saved sampling checks. Older
summaries without a checksum must be regenerated with the current bundled
\code{run.R} and \code{summarize.R}; no new sampling is needed. Keep the original
\code{model-data.rds} bytes with the fit when moving an export directory.

These checks detect mismatched files and inconsistent summaries; they do
not independently verify draws, convergence, prior adequacy or model fit.
The object contains marginal summaries, not joint posterior draws or a
native fitted model. It supports \code{\link[=mfrm_report]{mfrm_report()}}, \code{\link[=apa_table]{apa_table()}},
\code{\link[=build_summary_table_bundle]{build_summary_table_bundle()}} and \code{\link[=export_summary_appendix]{export_summary_appendix()}}.
Use the backend's saved fit for new credible levels, contrasts, predictions,
trace plots or posterior predictive checks. Native scoring and MML/JML
diagnostics are not available from this object.
Estimated-population one-family Stan summaries retain the population mean
and SD as reported parameters. Their schema-2 files require this newer reader;
existing fixed-population schema-1 summaries remain supported. Use
\code{plot(x, quantity = "population_sd")} to inspect between-Person variation
on the native metric. This SD is not an individual Person standard error.

\code{confint()} retrieves the stored equal-tailed 95\% \emph{posterior credible}
intervals, retaining fixed/unavailable rows and review flags. It does not
calculate frequentist confidence intervals or change the saved level.
Fixed anchors and constraint-determined values have no estimated SD or
interval. External calibration uncertainty has not been propagated.
\code{summary()} returns the complete validated summary; printing is compact.
The bundled synthetic example uses short chains and requires sampling
review. Its numbers are workflow illustrations, not assessment findings.

\code{plot()} method: Plot saved posterior estimates and credible intervals

For \code{plot()}: These are saved equal-tailed 95\% posterior credible intervals,
not frequentist confidence intervals. Dashed intervals and open circles
mark estimates whose sampling screen requires review; solid intervals
mean that no sampling flag was detected, not that inference is validated.
Open diamonds mark fixed anchors/constraint-determined values without an
interval. Crosses mark estimated points without an available interval.
Missing points retain their labels and are never replaced by zero.
Original row order, labels and endpoints are preserved. The plot never
refits, changes the level, selects significant effects or validates priors.
}
\section{Session plot defaults}{

Set \code{options(mfrmr.plot_preset = "publication")} to choose a session default
for plotting functions that expose the common \code{preset} argument. The
supported values are \code{"standard"}, \code{"publication"}, \code{"compact"} and
\code{"monochrome"}. Precedence is an explicit call argument, then the session
option, then \code{"standard"}. For example, \code{preset = "standard"} overrides
a session set to \code{"monochrome"}. Explicit \code{preset = NULL} retains the
earlier package-default behavior; it does not read the session option.
Invalid session values cause an error only when that option is needed.

The category-curve, data-quality, fit-review, connectivity and network
routes of \code{plot()} for report bundles use the same option through \code{...}.
Plots without a common \code{preset} argument, including extended-model plots
with their own \code{palette} controls, keep their own settings. This option
selects a preset, not a universal theme or a guarantee that all renderers
implement every appearance control identically.

New plot payloads retain the resolved preset for supported saved-data
rendering. Converting an existing payload with \code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} uses its saved
appearance, even after the session option changes. A call that creates a
new plot from a fit or statistical result uses the current default.
For a reproducible script, supply \code{preset} explicitly or set the option in
that script. Saving only the fitted model does not save a session option.
No global ggplot theme is changed.

Restore previous settings with \code{old <- options(mfrmr.plot_preset = "monochrome")} followed by \code{options(old)}. Use
\code{options(mfrmr.plot_preset = NULL)} to remove the option. The preset changes
appearance, not estimates, confidence levels or diagnostic thresholds.
}

